#!/usr/bin/env bash
# fusion-paths — resolve a consumer's workbench write and search targets
#
# Usage: fusion-paths <name> [<item-dir>]
#
# <name> is an agent (agents/<name>.md) or a skill (skills/<name>/SKILL.md).
# The two share one flat namespace: each consumer asks for its own keys under
# its own name — `fusion-paths coder`, `fusion-paths memo`.
#
# <item-dir> names the work item in scope, as a directory name that must
# already exist under circles/. It is how a dispatcher sends an agent into an
# item other than the one this checkout holds. With no second argument the item
# in scope is this checkout's own claim, read through bin/fusion-claimed-item —
# the single implementation of that criterion, which bin/fusion-rules also
# calls. This script does not re-derive it.
#
# Prints one KEY=value line per emitted key on stdout. The single resolution
# point for the workbench layout: no agent prompt and no skill body hard-codes
# a store path — the prompt says "write your plan to $OUT_PLAN", this script
# says what $OUT_PLAN is.
#
# The layout and the meaning of every key are defined in
# rules/fusion-workbench-conventions.md ("## fusion-workbench Layout",
# "## Path Resolution (Pfadauflösung)") and, for the key table and the name
# namespace, in rules/workbench-path-resolution.md. That first document and
# this script are two of the definition sites where a store-directory path
# literal may appear; the full set is enumerated as DEFINITION_SITES in
# hooks/lib/__tests__/path-literal-lint.test.ts, which enforces it.
#
# Called from Setup step 2, alongside `fusion-rules <agent>` — the step every
# agent demonstrably runs. Resolve once, use the values for the session.
#
# Output shape:
#   WORKBENCH   absolute path to fusion-workbench/
#   everything else  workbench-relative
#
# Exit codes (0/1/2 are the shape of bin/fusion-rules; 3 and 4 are this
# resolver's own). Callers key on the code, so no code carries two meanings:
#   0  success
#   1  usage error (missing name, more than two arguments, or an <item-dir>
#      that names no directory under circles/ — a caller's mistake, not a fault
#      in the workbench), or no workbench found above pwd
#   2  unknown name (no such agent and no such skill)
#   3  the item in scope cannot be determined: this checkout holds two or more
#      claimed items, or its own identifier could not be read inside a git work
#      tree. No output, and NEVER a fall back to shared/ — that answer is exit
#      0, and it is a different answer.
#   4  internal error: the prompt names a key this resolver cannot order or
#      value, or one name is both an agent and a skill
#      — a fusion BUG. The user cannot fix it by touching the workbench, and
#      must not be told to (see rules/fusion-workbench-conventions.md
#      "## Path Resolution" → Exit codes).
#
# EXIT 3 IS UNKNOWN SCOPE AND NOTHING ELSE. It is not the old orphaned-pointer
# code wearing a new meaning: that one said .active-circle was corrupt, and it
# went with the pointer. A caller that keys on the number gets the meaning the
# conventions state and no other. A project that is not a git work tree takes
# no claim at all, so no item in scope is the TRUE answer there and it exits 0
# into shared/ — which is why bin/fusion-identity splits its 3 and 5 from its 4
# and why neither this script nor bin/fusion-claimed-item merges them back.
# bin/fusion-rules also exits 3, for an unrelated reason, a malformed
# rules/context-manifest.yaml: read a 3 against the helper that returned it,
# never across the two.
#
# Resolution rules (from the conventions' "## Path Resolution"):
#   - Every OUT_* points into the container of the item in scope, and into
#     shared/ when there is none. That is the Origin Rule stated executably:
#     the placement question is answered once, here, at Setup, so a consumer
#     carries no branch and "no item in scope" is an answer it receives rather
#     than an error it has to handle.
#   - Every SCAN_* names BOTH stores for its kind, the container first and the
#     shared one second, and collapses to the shared one alone when no item is
#     in scope. An artifact filed under a directive is the same kind as one
#     filed without, so a consumer that read only the first value would report
#     a clean pass over half the corpus.
#   - Three kinds are unconditionally shared, because no container holds them:
#     consult/, memos/ and forum/ answer to nobody's directive. And circles/ IS
#     the container store, so OUT_BACKLOG and SCAN_BACKLOG name it whole rather
#     than naming anything inside one container.
#   - OUT_HISTORY and SCAN_HISTORY are LEGACY. The history store is closed to
#     writes (rules/fusion-workbench-conventions.md "## Session history"): no
#     agent prompt names either key, the existing corpus stays on disk and stays
#     readable, and SCAN_HISTORY is emitted for /fusion:cadence, which digests
#     that frozen corpus. Both arms stay valued only while a shipped prompt
#     still names them — removing an arm a prompt names is exit 4, not silence,
#     which is why the arm outlives the last write rather than preceding it.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Why the key sets are derived and not declared
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Emission is per-consumer, as in bin/fusion-rules: each gets only the keys it
# needs — a coder gets no OUT_PLAN, an editor gets no OUT_ISSUE. What each
# needs is READ OUT OF ITS PROMPT, not declared here.
#
# The declared version failed twice. The sets were built in T2-A by a
# deliberate line-by-line audit of all 15 prompts, and that audit went 14/15:
# it missed that reconciler files decision records, so $OUT_DECISION expanded
# empty and every record it filed landed at the workbench root. Silent — the
# write succeeded, in the wrong place. Found only when P-6 converted the
# prompt; fixed in 45d8a71. The problem was never care. A declared set is a
# second copy of what the prompt already says, and every prompt edit re-rolls
# the dice on the copy (HYG-SOT).
#
# T2-A judged mechanical derivation impossible, and it was: a pre-conversion
# prompt named a directory in prose, and no grep separates "Skim
# fusion-workbench/issues/" (a read) from "file to fusion-workbench/issues/"
# (a write) without judgment. P-4..P-7 removed that obstacle by converting the
# prompts. `$SCAN_ISSUES` vs `$OUT_ISSUE` in the prompt body IS the read/write
# marker now, and it is machine-readable. So the prompt is the single source,
# and the rule the conventions state — every directory a consumer reads needs a
# SCAN_* key, every kind it writes needs an OUT_* — holds by construction
# instead of by audit.
#
# Derivation is at run time, not generated into this file at build time:
#   - It costs ONE grep over ONE file. The set is derived for the requested
#     name only, so `fusion-paths coder` reads agents/coder.md and nothing
#     else. (The cost feared when this was decided — "a grep over 26 files on
#     every call" — assumed all sets were built per call. They are not.)
#   - A generated block would be a third artifact to maintain and ship, and it
#     would go stale exactly the way the declared sets did: whenever someone
#     edits a prompt and does not re-run the generator. That is the same defect
#     with an extra step.
#
# This diverges from bin/fusion-rules, which hand-maintains its agent → rule
# pattern mapping, and the divergence is deliberate. fusion-rules has no source
# to derive from: an agent's prompt does not name the rule-file patterns that
# apply to it, so its mapping is a genuine authored fact. This resolver's key
# set is not a fact — it is a restatement of the prompt. The two scripts stay
# symmetric where symmetry was the point (a required name argument, KEY=value
# on stdout,
# same Setup step, same 0/1/2 exit shape); only the internal table differs,
# because only one of them has a source of truth to read.
#
# What derivation gives up: over-emission — a key emitted that no prompt names
# — becomes structurally impossible, and with it the signal it carried. That
# signal was real but misfiled. When a declared set held a key the prompt never
# used, the finding was usually "this prompt is missing a step" (planner names
# issue entries with no path), not "this key is spare". A prompt gap is found
# by reading the prompt. It was only visible here by accident, as the diff
# between a human's belief about a prompt and the prompt's text — and it cost
# the under-emission defect above to keep. The eleven known cases are recorded
# as prompt-gap findings in
# 260717-0031_*_p8-lint-gate-scope-open-questions-from-conversions.md
# so they survive this change rather than dissolving into it.
#
# Consequence for P-8's lint gate: under- and over-emission both leave its
# scope. Its remaining job is the one it was really for — no store-path literal
# in a prompt.
#
# Note on skills: a skill passes its OWN name. `/fusion:memo` resolves
# `fusion-paths memo` and gets OUT_MEMO from skills/memo/SKILL.md. The one
# exception is `/fusion:setup`, which is the orchestrator's Setup procedure
# factored into a skill: its Step 2 resolves values the ORCHESTRATOR holds for
# the whole session, so it asks for `orchestrator` and is right to.

set -eu

NAME="${1:-}"
if [ -z "$NAME" ]; then
  echo "Usage: $0 <name> [<item-dir>]   # <name> is an agent (agents/<name>.md) or a skill (skills/<name>/SKILL.md); <item-dir> is a work item's directory under circles/" >&2
  exit 1
fi

if [ "$#" -gt 2 ]; then
  echo "fusion-paths: expected at most two arguments (<name> [<item-dir>]), got $#." >&2
  exit 1
fi

# The second argument is present or absent, and an empty string is neither: it
# would read as absent and silently resolve the claim instead of the item the
# caller meant to name. `$#` is what tells them apart.
ITEM_ARG=""
HAVE_ITEM_ARG=0
if [ "$#" -eq 2 ]; then
  HAVE_ITEM_ARG=1
  ITEM_ARG="$2"
fi

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 1. Resolve the name to exactly one prompt file, and derive its key set.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
SCRIPT_DIR="$(cd "$(dirname "$0")" && pwd -P)"
PLUGIN_ROOT="$(dirname "$SCRIPT_DIR")"

# Repo-preference: when cwd is the fusion plugin's own source repo, resolve
# prompts from the WORK TREE rather than from this script's install location.
# This script is normally invoked as $FUSION_PLUGIN_ROOT/bin/fusion-paths —
# the installed copy — whose agents/ and skills/ can be sessions stale against
# the prompts being edited in the repo, and the key set would then be derived
# from an outdated prompt. The prompt files are the ONLY plugin-root-relative
# resources this script reads, so this line is the whole of the preference.
# Criterion shared with bin/fusion-rules via the sibling helper
# bin/fusion-plugin-cwd, the only cwd-anchored implementation of it
# (hooks/lib/self-detect.ts keeps the root-anchored `isFusionPluginRoot(dir)`
# and no cwd entry point). Decision
# 260806-0015_*_veraltete-regeln-im-eigenen-repo-melden-oder-umgehen, option c.
if "$SCRIPT_DIR/fusion-plugin-cwd"; then
  PLUGIN_ROOT="$PWD"
fi

# Names are flat lowercase slugs. The guard is a safety property, not a style
# rule: the name is interpolated into a path below, so anything with a slash or
# a dot segment could read a file outside agents/ and skills/.
#
# POSIX classes, not `[!a-z0-9-]`: a range in a case pattern is collation-based,
# and under most locales `a-z` also matches uppercase. That let `Coder` through
# — and on a case-insensitive filesystem it then resolved to agents/coder.md,
# so the guard was passing for the wrong reason on the machine most likely to
# run it.
case "$NAME" in
  *[![:lower:][:digit:]-]*)
    echo "fusion-paths: unknown name '$NAME'. A name is an agent (agents/<name>.md) or a skill (skills/<name>/SKILL.md), and is a lowercase slug." >&2
    exit 2
    ;;
esac

AGENT_PROMPT="$PLUGIN_ROOT/agents/$NAME.md"
SKILL_PROMPT="$PLUGIN_ROOT/skills/$NAME/SKILL.md"

if [ -f "$AGENT_PROMPT" ] && [ -f "$SKILL_PROMPT" ]; then
  # Agents and skills share one flat namespace, so a name that is both has two
  # prompts and two key sets, and no basis to prefer either. Silently picking
  # one would hand a consumer another consumer's paths. No collision exists
  # today; this fires the moment someone creates one, at which point the fix is
  # a rename.
  echo "fusion-paths: internal error — '$NAME' is both an agent (agents/$NAME.md) and a skill (skills/$NAME/SKILL.md). The namespace is flat, so the key set is ambiguous. Rename one of them. This is a bug in fusion, not a fault in your workbench." >&2
  exit 4
elif [ -f "$AGENT_PROMPT" ]; then
  PROMPT="$AGENT_PROMPT"
elif [ -f "$SKILL_PROMPT" ]; then
  PROMPT="$SKILL_PROMPT"
else
  echo "fusion-paths: unknown name '$NAME'. Expected an agent (agents/$NAME.md) or a skill (skills/$NAME/SKILL.md)." >&2
  exit 2
fi

# The prompt's own `$OUT_*` / `$SCAN_*` references ARE the key set. WORKBENCH is
# deliberately not matched: it is emitted unconditionally, so it is not part of
# any consumer's set.
#
# `$OUT_*` and `$SCAN_*` appear literally in the prompts' boilerplate ("use
# them wherever a later step names a `$OUT_*` value"). They do not match: the
# pattern requires a letter after the underscore, and `*` is not one.
#
# A consumer whose prompt names no key derives an empty set and receives
# WORKBENCH alone. That is a true answer, not a failure — /fusion:help and
# /fusion:commit file nothing.
KEYS="$(grep -oE '\$(OUT|SCAN)_[A-Z][A-Z_]*' "$PROMPT" | tr -d '$' | sort -u | tr '\n' ' ')"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2. Locate the workbench. Delegated to bin/fusion-workbench-root — the single
#    source of truth for "is this project fusion-set-up, and where".
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
if ! PROJECT_ROOT="$("$SCRIPT_DIR/fusion-workbench-root")"; then
  echo "fusion-paths: no fusion workbench found above $(pwd). Run /fusion:setup first." >&2
  exit 1
fi
WORKBENCH="$PROJECT_ROOT/fusion-workbench"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 3. Resolve the item in scope. This is the whole of the workbench state this
#    script reads, and it is read once.
#
#    Two ways in. A caller that NAMES an item overrides everything: it is how a
#    dispatcher sends an agent into an item other than the one this checkout
#    holds, and the named directory must already exist, because a container is
#    created by whoever files the item and not as a side effect of resolving a
#    path. A caller that names none gets this checkout's own claim.
#
#    The criterion behind that claim is NOT implemented here. It lives once, in
#    bin/fusion-claimed-item, which bin/fusion-rules calls from the same Setup
#    step for its own reason. Two implementations of one criterion drift, and
#    the drift is invisible because each answers plausibly on its own.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
SCOPE=""

if [ "$HAVE_ITEM_ARG" = 1 ]; then
  # The argument is interpolated into a path, so the shape guard is a safety
  # property and not a style rule — the same reason <name> carries one above.
  case "$ITEM_ARG" in
    ""|.|..|*/*|*[!A-Za-z0-9._-]*)
      echo "fusion-paths: '$ITEM_ARG' is not a work item directory name. Pass the directory's own name, as it stands under circles/ — not a path, and not empty." >&2
      exit 1
      ;;
  esac
  if [ ! -d "$WORKBENCH/circles/$ITEM_ARG" ]; then
    # Exit 1 and not 3: the scope is perfectly determinable, the caller just
    # named an item that is not there. Nothing in the workbench is at fault and
    # the user must not be sent to repair it.
    echo "fusion-paths: no work item directory 'circles/$ITEM_ARG' under $WORKBENCH. The second argument names an item that must already exist." >&2
    exit 1
  fi
  SCOPE="circles/$ITEM_ARG"
else
  CLAIMED="$SCRIPT_DIR/fusion-claimed-item"
  if [ ! -x "$CLAIMED" ]; then
    # Not shared/. A missing sibling means the question was never asked, which
    # is unknown scope; answering shared/ here would file an item's work outside
    # its container and say nothing. An install carries both helpers or neither,
    # so this is an incomplete install rather than a version skew.
    echo "fusion-paths: $CLAIMED is missing, so the item in scope is unknown. The plugin install is incomplete." >&2
    exit 3
  fi

  # Its stderr is kept back on a clean answer and passed through on every other:
  # "not a git work tree" is a TRUE answer and would otherwise print on every
  # agent's Setup in a project that has no git, while the two failure messages
  # name the files the user has to fix and must reach them.
  CLAIM_ERR="$(mktemp)"
  trap 'rm -f "$CLAIM_ERR"' EXIT
  set +e
  CLAIM_OUT="$("$CLAIMED" 2>"$CLAIM_ERR")"
  CLAIM_RC=$?
  set -e

  if [ "$CLAIM_RC" -ne 0 ]; then
    cat "$CLAIM_ERR" >&2
  fi

  case "$CLAIM_RC" in
    0)
      # Exit 0 with no output is "no item claimed", and it is an answer, not a
      # degradation: it is what puts a consumer's writes in the shared store.
      SCOPE="$(printf '%s\n' "$CLAIM_OUT" | sed -n 's/^CONTAINER=//p' | head -n 1)"
      ;;
    1)
      # The helper's only stop code, passed straight through with its own reason
      # already on stderr.
      echo "fusion-paths: bin/fusion-claimed-item stopped, so nothing is resolved." >&2
      exit 1
      ;;
    *)
      # 3, and anything the helper may grow that this script does not know: both
      # are unknown scope. The one thing no caller may do with a non-zero is
      # read it as "none claimed" — that is exit 0, and it is a different
      # answer.
      echo "fusion-paths: the item in scope cannot be determined (bin/fusion-claimed-item exited $CLAIM_RC), so no path is resolved and nothing falls back to shared/." >&2
      exit 3
      ;;
  esac
fi

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 4. Resolve values. One kind, two candidate stores, and section 3 already
#    decided between them: OUT_BASE is the container of the item in scope, or
#    shared/ when there is none, so the write table below carries no branch of
#    its own. The read table carries exactly one, in scan_value().
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
if [ -n "$SCOPE" ]; then
  OUT_BASE="$SCOPE"
else
  OUT_BASE="shared"
fi

scan_value() {
  # $1 = the kind's subdirectory name. Both stores when an item is in scope,
  # container first; the shared store alone when none is. The order is part of
  # the contract: a consumer that shows the first hit shows the item's own.
  if [ -n "$SCOPE" ]; then
    printf '%s %s' "$SCOPE/$1" "shared/$1"
  else
    printf '%s' "shared/$1"
  fi
}

value_for() {
  case "$1" in
    OUT_PLAN)                     printf '%s' "$OUT_BASE/planning" ;;
    OUT_HISTORY)                  printf '%s' "$OUT_BASE/history" ;;
    OUT_ISSUE)                    printf '%s' "$OUT_BASE/issues" ;;
    OUT_DECISION)                 printf '%s' "$OUT_BASE/decisions" ;;
    OUT_REVIEW)                   printf '%s' "$OUT_BASE/reviews" ;;
    OUT_ANALYSIS)                 printf '%s' "$OUT_BASE/analyses" ;;
    SCAN_PLANS)                   scan_value "planning" ;;
    SCAN_HISTORY)                 scan_value "history" ;;
    SCAN_ISSUES)                  scan_value "issues" ;;
    SCAN_DECISIONS)               scan_value "decisions" ;;
    SCAN_REVIEWS)                 scan_value "reviews" ;;
    SCAN_ANALYSES)                scan_value "analyses" ;;
    # Unconditionally shared: a consultation, a memo and a message answer to
    # nobody's directive, so no container holds them and the Origin Rule has
    # nothing to decide.
    OUT_CONSULT)                  printf '%s' "shared/consult" ;;
    OUT_FORUM|SCAN_FORUM)         printf '%s' "shared/forum" ;;
    # The container store itself, whole. An item's record lives inside its own
    # container, so the pair names circles/ and never one directory in it.
    OUT_BACKLOG|SCAN_BACKLOG)     printf '%s' "circles" ;;
    # Two kinds have a write key and no read key, and it is the same criterion
    # both times: a key set restates the prompts, so a key no prompt names
    # restates nothing. Nothing reads memos — a memo is written for the user.
    # And SCAN_CONSULT lost its last consumer on 2026-09-10, when the archive
    # skill's shared-store derivation went; the store and its reports stay.
    OUT_MEMO)                     printf '%s' "shared/memos" ;;
    *)
      # Reached when a key is in ORDER but has no value here — the second half
      # of the same guarantee the ORDER check below enforces. Between them:
      # a derived key must be in ORDER and valued here, or the run stops.
      # Neither can be forgotten silently.
      # Loud rather than silent, so a key added without a value cannot ship as
      # an empty string and send an agent's writes to the workbench root.
      echo "fusion-paths: internal error — no value defined for key '$1'. This is a bug in fusion-paths, not a fault in your workbench." >&2
      exit 4
      ;;
  esac
}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 5. Emit. Canonical order, filtered by the derived key set.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
ORDER="OUT_PLAN OUT_HISTORY OUT_ISSUE OUT_DECISION OUT_REVIEW OUT_ANALYSIS
       OUT_CONSULT OUT_MEMO OUT_BACKLOG OUT_FORUM
       SCAN_PLANS SCAN_ISSUES SCAN_DECISIONS SCAN_HISTORY SCAN_REVIEWS
       SCAN_ANALYSES SCAN_BACKLOG SCAN_FORUM"

has_key() {
  local want="$1" k
  for k in $KEYS; do
    [ "$k" = "$want" ] && return 0
  done
  return 1
}

# Emission is driven by ORDER, not by KEYS, so a derived key absent from ORDER
# is never looked up — it does not reach value_for, and the unreachable-branch
# guard there never fires. It simply vanishes: the consumer is not given the
# key, its prompt expands $SCAN_FOO to the empty string, and the write lands at
# the workbench root. Silent, and exactly the failure mode the value_for guard
# exists to prevent, arrived at one step earlier.
#
# Derivation changes what this catches but not that it is needed. The key can
# no longer be mistyped HERE — it is read from the prompt — so what it now
# catches is a key mistyped THERE ($SCAN_ISUES), or a genuinely new key
# introduced in a prompt before this resolver learned to value it. Both are
# authoring errors in the same class, and both stop the run rather than
# quietly dropping a path.
for key in $KEYS; do
  found=0
  for known in $ORDER; do
    [ "$key" = "$known" ] && { found=1; break; }
  done
  if [ "$found" = 0 ]; then
    echo "fusion-paths: internal error — '$PROMPT' names \$$key, which this resolver does not know. Either it is a typo in the prompt, or the key is new and must be added to ORDER and to value_for() in $0. This is a bug in fusion, not a fault in your workbench." >&2
    exit 4
  fi
done

# Build the whole output before writing any of it. A failure mid-loop would
# otherwise leave a partial key set on stdout alongside the error — and a
# caller that reads stdout without checking the exit code would act on half a
# contract. Every non-zero exit yields no output at all.

# WORKBENCH is always emitted, and is the only absolute path.
OUT="$(printf 'WORKBENCH=%s\n' "$WORKBENCH")"

for key in $ORDER; do
  if has_key "$key"; then
    # Assign first, then append: under `set -e` a failing value_for aborts the
    # run rather than emitting `KEY=` with an empty value.
    val="$(value_for "$key")"
    OUT="$OUT
$key=$val"
  fi
done

printf '%s\n' "$OUT"
